## The model never sees a request

<At levels="simple">

When you type into a chat, it feels like asking a question. To the model it's the start of a
document, and its only job is to guess what comes next (<ChapterRef to="guessing-game" />). Every
word you write changes those guesses. <GlossaryPopover term="prompt-engineering">Prompt engineering</GlossaryPopover>
means choosing your words so that the answer you want is the most likely next text.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

When you type into a chat, it feels like asking a question. To the model it is the start of a
document, and its only job is to guess what comes next (<ChapterRef to="guessing-game" />). The prompt is the context (<ChapterRef to="the-context-problem" />), and
every word in it pulls the probabilities of the next token one way or another (<ChapterRef to="probability-and-sampling" />). <GlossaryPopover term="prompt-engineering">Prompt engineering</GlossaryPopover>
is choosing that text so that the answer you want becomes the likely continuation.

</At>

You can watch this happen with GPT-2, which has had no training to follow instructions. Change one
part of the prompt at a time:

<PromptVariants />

## Setting the scene

<At levels="simple">

After "The cat sat on the", GPT-2 guesses *floor* about 8% of the time. Start with "It was
bedtime," and *bed* jumps from 7% to 22%. Put the cat at the beach, and *sand* becomes one of its
top guesses. The shading shows which earlier words the last one looked at (<ChapterRef to="attention" />).

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

"The cat sat on the" ends with *floor* about 8% of the time. Add "It was bedtime," in front, and
*bed* jumps from about 7% to 22%. Put the cat at the beach, and *sand*, nearly impossible before,
becomes one of the top guesses. The shading shows why: attention (<ChapterRef to="attention" />) lets the last token pull in
"kitchen" or "beach" from the start of the sentence.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Where the rest of the attention goes

The shading leaves out the first token, because it would drown out everything else. Averaged over
GPT-2's 144 heads (12 in each of 12 layers), the last token of "The cat sat on the" puts 63% of its
attention on "The", and across the eight prompts here the first token takes between 46% and 91%.
That isn't because "The" matters. A head's attention weights come from a softmax, so they must add
up to 1, and a head with nothing useful to find still has to put its weight somewhere. Models
learn to park it on the first token, which every later token can see. Researchers call this an
*attention sink*, and found that keeping its keys and values is what lets a model that only keeps
a short window of recent tokens go on working through very long text.<Cite id="xiao2024" /> The
widget sets the first token aside and rescales the rest to add up to 1.

</At>

## Making the answer the obvious next text

<At levels="simple">

Ask GPT-2 "What is the capital of France?" and it gives *Paris* only half a percent: its favorite
next step is a new line. Write "The capital of France is" instead, and *Paris* becomes about six
times as likely, because now the answer is the obvious next word.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Asking GPT-2 "What is the capital of France?" gives *Paris* only half a percent; its top guess, at
34%, is a new line. Writing the prompt as the start of an answer, "The capital of France is", makes
*Paris* about six times as likely. The "Q: … A:" layout works the same way: after "A:", an answer is
what comes next.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Conditioning, written down

Written out, the model gives $p(x_{t+1} \mid x_1, \ldots, x_t)$, and a prompt is just the first
tokens of $x$. The weights stay fixed, so the whole move from 0.54% to 3.2% for " Paris" comes from
changing those tokens. A number like that counts only one way of writing the answer, though. After
"Q: What is the capital of France?" and "A:", the top guess is " The", at 23%, far ahead of
" Paris" at 1.8%: many of those replies would go on to say "The capital of France is Paris". The
chance that the answer appears at all adds up over every reply that contains it, which no single
next-token probability shows.

</At>

<At levels="simple">

Chat models were tuned so that a plain question is already followed by an answer (<ChapterRef to="predictor-to-assistant" />), so they
need less of this coaxing. It still works the same way: your words set up the document, and the
model continues it.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Chat models were tuned so that a plain question is already followed by an answer (<ChapterRef to="predictor-to-assistant" />), so they
need less of this coaxing. The mechanism is the same: your words set up the document, and the model
continues it. GPT-3 showed how far a large enough model can go on this alone, doing new tasks from a
description and a few examples in the prompt, with no change to its weights.<Cite id="brown2020" />

</At>

<RecapCard>

- A prompt is the start of a document; the model predicts what comes next.
- Every word shifts the next-token probabilities, through attention to the earlier text.
- A good prompt makes the answer you want the most likely continuation.

<At levels="deep">

- Most attention parks on the first token, a sink; a next-token probability counts only one way
  of writing the answer.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** instead of describing a task, could you just show the model a few examples?
